    "lawCode": "Add §280-c, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2763A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public health law, in relation\nto audits of pharmacies\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo establish statutory guidelines regarding the audit of pharmacy\nrecords conducted on the behest of or ordered by a pharmacy benefit\nmanager, and to provide guidance to a pharmacy benefit manager\nregarding the denial or payment of claims.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of this bill adds a new section 280-c to the Public Health\nLaw. This new section would require pharmacy benefit managers, when\nconducting an audit of that looks to audit pharmacy records to:\n\n*Schedule on-site audit of a pharmacy at any time other than during\nthe first three calendar days of any month.\n\n*Notify the pharmacy that an audit is pending at least seven days\nbefore the on-site audit begins. Notice is required to be written and\nif sent by common carrier, return receipt must be requested. If the\nnotice is sent electronically,\n\n*Limit audit periods to 24 months after the date a claim is submitted.\n\n*Provide written advance notice of the specific prescription numbers\nthat as included in the audit. The prescription numbers may exclude\nthe last 2 digits.\n\n*Accept written and verifiable records to validate pharmacy records.\n\n*Limit audits to 100 randomly selected prescriptions within a 12-month\nperiod, unless there is fraud.\n\n*Provide the affected pharmacy with a copy of a preliminary audit\nreport within 45 days of the audit completion.\n\n*If a remote or desk audit reveals the necessity to review additional\nclaims, the follow-up audit may be conducted on-site.\n\n*Provide the pharmacy a way to provide documentation to contest audit\nfindings and allow for the consideration of an extension of time to\nsubmit the documentation or contest the accuracy of the report.\n\n*Provide the pharmacy with the final audit report within 60 days of\nreceipt of the initial report.\n\nThe bill also would also require a pharmacy benefit manager to pay a\nclaim submitted by a pharmacy if the initial reason for denial is tied\nto a clerical or other non-intentional error if the relevant\nprescription was still effectively filled.\n\nThe audit requirements would not apply to an audit initiated on the\nreasonable suspicion of deliberate fraud, or audits of claims paid by\nfederal programs such as Medicaid or Medicare, or timely routine desk\naudits where no chargeback or recoupment are demanded.\n\nSection two of this bill provides that this act shall take effect on\nthe sixtieth day after it shall have become a law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\n\nThe bill is designed to limit the ability of pharmacy benefit managers\nto use of audit procedures or payment denials to inhibit normal\nbusiness activities of pharmacies of whatever size and nature, and to\nprovide a more level competitive playing field for all pharmacies.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2016: S.7150 - Passed the Senate\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nUndetermined\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
